##.m2 and settings.xml


“.m2/repository is the cache of downloaded dependencies, and 
settings.xml next to it holds the machine-level configuration: proxy, mirrors, repository credentials and the local repository path. 
The pom.xml describes the project, while settings.xml describes the environment, so the same project builds on any machine or network.”




 ##settings.xml - Why it matters for your framework ??
 
On a corporate network, builds can fail with “could not transfer artifact” until the proxy or mirror is set here.
Jenkins agents use it for repository credentials, so passwords stay out of the pom.xml.


<-- It holds settings about your machine and network, not about a project (that’s what pom.xml is for). The main sections: -->

Section	Purpose
<localRepository>	Moves the local repository (the repository folder you opened) to another path or drive
<proxies>	Proxy host, port and login, needed on a corporate network
<mirrors>	Redirects downloads to a company Nexus/Artifactory instead of Maven Central
<servers>	Usernames and passwords for private repositories (matched by <id>)
<profiles> / <activeProfiles>


<settings>
  <localRepository>D:/maven-repo</localRepository>

  <proxies>
    <proxy>
      <id>office-proxy</id>
      <active>true</active>
      <protocol>http</protocol>
      <host>proxy.company.com</host>
      <port>8080</port>
    </proxy>
  </proxies>

  <mirrors>
    <mirror>
      <id>company-nexus</id>
      <mirrorOf>*</mirrorOf>
      <url>https://nexus.company.com/repository/maven-public/</url>
    </mirror>
  </mirrors>
</settings>
